WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Normalization.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:07.167
Squeezing a feature into zero to one is simple, but it has one dangerous weakness.

3
00:00:08.367 --> 00:00:14.433
Normalization, also called min-max scaling, squeezes a feature into the range zero to one.

4
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:19.000
On top, we subtract the minimum, so the smallest value becomes zero.

5
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.633
Underneath, we divide by the range, so the largest value becomes one.

6
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:26.633
Everything else lands proportionally in between.

7
00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:32.900
Here are five planets with orbital periods from three point five to four hundred days.

8
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.033
The shortest orbit becomes zero.

9
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:38.667
The longest becomes one, and the rest spread out in between.

10
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.367
Now watch what one extreme outlier does.

11
00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:48.000
A single planet with a hundred thousand day orbit becomes the new maximum, so it sits at one.

12
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.300
Every normal planet is crushed into the range zero to point zero zero four.

13
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.767
The feature is effectively destroyed.

14
00:00:56.967 --> 00:00:58.633
Here is the same thing in code.

15
00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:02.233
By hand, subtract the minimum and divide by the range.

16
00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:05.833
The scikit-learn scaler gives exactly the same numbers.

17
00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.933
But add the rogue orbit, and the real planets all collapse near zero.

18
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.333
This really happens.

19
00:01:12.633 --> 00:01:18.367
One directly imaged planet in the exoplanet table has an orbit of a hundred thousand days.

20
00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:23.221
After min-max scaling, orbital period looks constant for nearly every row,

21
00:01:23.221 --> 00:01:25.867
and a nearest neighbor baseline ignores it.

22
00:01:26.167 --> 00:01:28.313
The fix is to take the log first,

23
00:01:28.313 --> 00:01:32.671
or use a robust scaler built on the median and interquartile range.

24
00:01:32.671 --> 00:01:36.833
Plain standardization still lets the outlier inflate the spread.

25
00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:38.849
Let's recap.

26
00:01:38.849 --> 00:01:41.567
Min-max maps a feature into zero to one.

27
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:45.533
It suits features that must stay in a fixed range, like pixels.

28
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:47.967
But one outlier crushes everyone else.

29
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:50.928
So know your minimum and maximum first.

30
00:01:50.928 --> 00:01:52.633
Next up: standardization.
